{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 25,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the commissioned-sales arrangement and Lovejoy’s decision, after annual renewal, to eliminate most commissions through House Account reclassification rather than agreed termination."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Understanding the listed defects requires familiar whole-contract interpretation and implied-good-faith principles, not power-transmission expertise or the PCSRA’s specialised coverage rules."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The construction requires reading paragraph 7(c)’s reclassification power against paragraph 16’s termination scheme and paragraph 2’s continuing noncompetition restraint."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish the implied limitation on House Account discretion from the unresolved factual question of bad faith and the separately waived constructive-termination theory."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The defects share lines 71–78 and describe overlapping aspects of the same coherence problem, viewed through the noncompetition, House Account, and termination provisions."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02: It requires integrating the House Account discretion with both the termination scheme and the noncompetition restraint. The reviewer must understand that the court imposed a good-faith limitation on that discretion without finally deciding whether Lovejoy’s conduct breached it.",
  "reason": "This is suitable for a general commercial-contract reviewer with time for a focused cross-provision reading. The commercial dispute is straightforward, but the listed defects concern the relationship among three provisions rather than an isolated ambiguous phrase. The court explains that relationship plainly, although the reviewer must keep the surviving good-faith claim separate from the waived constructive-termination theory. The main recognition challenge is that the defect descriptions overlap substantially and identify different contractual perspectives on the same interpretive problem."
}
